              title: "Leak on Palmer Road — council says pipes, not vandalism",
              source: "From the Hackney Gazette (adapted)",
              text: "A leak in the basement of 14 Palmer Road is being investigated, the council said yesterday, after residents reported water in the hallway for the second time this month. It is thought that the leak started on Monday. It should be noted that nobody has confirmed a cause. Old pipes are being blamed in early reports; vandalism has not been ruled out, a spokesperson said, and should not be assumed.<br/><br/>According to the council, residents had been warned twice, by notice, that work on the rising main was overdue. A letter is said to have been sent in March. Several neighbours told this paper they had not seen it. «Nobody was consulted,» said one resident, who asked not to be named. The claim has not been independently verified.<br/><br/>It is reported that a similar leak last winter led to a partial closure of the street. That closure was lifted after three days. The present leak is not expected to require a closure, the spokesperson said, provided that the water can be shut off overnight. The plan should be reviewed, a local councillor argued, because two warnings in a hallway are not the same as a conversation.<br/><br/>Mr Patel, whose shop sits on the corner, was offered a quote in this paper and declined. These claims — that the shop is closing, that the lease has been sold — have been denied. It is widely believed on a neighbourhood chat that the shop will shut if the leak is not fixed. It is understood that talks with the landlord are ongoing. The rumour cannot be proved, and this paper will not print it as fact.<br/><br/>A front-page treatment has been avoided on purpose. The leak is real. The cause is not yet known. Attribution, in this case, stops at the council and at residents who would go on the record. Unnamed sources on a chat are not a substitute. The evidence suggests a leak, not a conspiracy, and not yet a sale.<br/><br/>On balance, the story is smaller than the chat. Water in a hallway is a failure of maintenance. It is not, on the information available yesterday, a reason to close a shop that was taking deliveries this morning. Further updates will be published when a cause has been confirmed. Until then, the leak must not be ignored, and the sale need not be invented.<br/><br/>A note on method, because readers have asked how this paper decides what to print. A claim that cannot be attributed is not run as fact. A claim that can be attributed to the council, or to a resident willing to go on the record, is run with that name attached. It is widely believed on the chat that this standard is a form of cowardice. It can be argued that it is the only standard that still distinguishes a newspaper from a mood. The editor, who has a byline on this page, will answer letters. Unnamed sources on a group are invited to become named ones, in writing, by Friday. A second leak, if it comes, will be treated the same way: reported, attributed, and not dressed as a sale until a sale exists. That is slower than a chat. It is also still the job. Readers who prefer speed are not the enemy of this page. They are the reason the page has to be slower on purpose. A hallway can flood in an hour. A sale, if it is real, will still be real on Monday, when a name can be put under it. Who says so? Anyone this paper can print.",
