              title: "They're covering it up — share this",
              source: "From the Palmer Road neighbourhood group (adapted)",
              text: "It is widely believed on this street that the shop is being closed on purpose. A leak was «found» on Monday. Residents had been «warned», they say. Nobody saw a letter. The leak was allowed to run because it suits someone. Who? The landlord is a notorious name on this road. The shop is said to have been sold already. Patel was offered a quiet exit and took it. That is what is being said, and if the Gazette will not print it, we will.<br/><br/>A front-page story should have been written yesterday. Instead we got pipes. LEAK BLAMED ON OLD PIPES — blamed by who? An unstated agent, a council quote, a denial. Of course the claims have been denied. They are always denied. Denial is not evidence. The evidence is the water in the hall and the van that was seen at the back of the shop at six in the morning, which nobody from the paper has asked about.<br/><br/>It is reported, if you believe the Gazette, that talks are ongoing. Talks with who? It is understood that the lease is gone. Apparently the night bus is being cut the same month, which is not a coincidence. Word is the whole corner is being emptied for something else. They cancelled a residents' meeting last night. It got cancelled, they said, because of the leak. The leak is the excuse. The meeting is what they did not want.<br/><br/>People who defend the paper will tell you it is being careful. Careful is a word that covers people. The group is not paid to be careful. The group is here. We were not consulted. We were not offered a meeting that survived until morning. We were told to wait. Waiting is how this street loses shops.<br/><br/>Share this. Ask the editor why a byline was more important than a van. Ask Patel, if he is still here, why the deliveries looked like emptying. The rumour cannot be proved, they will say. Neither can the pipes, until someone names the person who decided to let the water run. Who says so? We do. On the record, without a suit.<br/><br/>And if the Gazette wants a named source, here is one. This street has lived with a notorious delay on the rising main for two winters. Notices in a hallway are not consultation. A letter said to have been sent in March is not a conversation. We were told to wait. We were not offered a date that survived until morning. Careful, as a word, has covered this corner long enough. The passive will not cover it any more. Print the van. Print the meeting that got cancelled. Print who decided. If that is not journalism, it is at least a record. We are done hiding the agent. Print the date of the letter that nobody saw. Print the name on the van. Print whether the night bus is being cut in the same month, and by whom. Apparently is not a source. Word is not a source. A street that has been told to wait is a source, if someone will listen without first asking for a password. We have been careful for two winters. Careful did not fix the main. Careful did not keep the meeting. Careful is the word the paper uses when it has decided not to name the person who benefits. Name them. If they have not sold the shop, they can say so on the record, like Patel, and we will print that too. Until then, the unstated agent is the whole story on this street.",
