              text: "Local newspapers in Britain are not dying because nobody cares about leaks in hallways. They are dying because a neighbourhood group can publish a rumour in four minutes, and a reporter with a byline cannot. The reporter has to ring the council, wait for a quote, and write «it is thought that» when the quote does not come. The group writes «it is widely believed» and hits send. Both sentences are passive. Only one of them will still be true in the morning.<br/><br/>This is not a defence of slowness. It is a description of two machines. The paper's machine is built to survive a denial. If Patel says the shop is not closing, the paper has to print the denial or drop the claim. The group's machine is built to survive a feeling. If Patel denies it, the denial becomes evidence that he would say that, wouldn't he. Same grammar. Opposite job.<br/><br/>Readers are not fools. They know the difference between a source and a mood. They still tap the faster sentence, because the faster sentence is on the phone they already have open, and the paper is behind a website that asks for an email. IT in the community, again: the queue has moved. A leak that is being investigated is less shareable than a leak that is being covered up. The second version has an enemy. The first has a spokesperson.<br/><br/>It should be noted that some group posts are careful, and some papers are not. A Gazette that leads with LEAK BLAMED ON OLD PIPES is doing the group's work with better spelling. A resident who writes «according to the notice in the hall, work is overdue» is doing the paper's work without being paid. The passive does not belong to either side. The attribution does.<br/><br/>What would actually slow a rumour is not a lecture about grammar. It is a council that answers the phone, a shopkeeper who will go on the record, and a paper that still has an editor on Friday night. Until those exist, it can be argued that the group is the public sphere this street has left. That is not the same as saying the group is right. The evidence still suggests a leak, not a sale. Who says so? Anyone who rang Patel, or Ben, or the council, instead of hitting share.<br/><br/>A last distinction, because it is the one the exam will ask without using the word. A sentence that informs can be checked in the morning. A sentence that convinces is designed not to be. It is thought that the leak started on Monday: that can be checked. It is widely believed that the shop is being closed on purpose: that can only be believed. Readers who cannot tell the two apart will pass a B1 paper and fail this one. The grammar is the same. The job is not. That is why this part of the exam changed: not to add a gap, but to take the gap away, and to see whether you can still tell a report from a mood when both of them fit in one long page.",
