              title: "Part-time undergraduate study: regulations at a glance",
              source: "Camden Adult College, Applicant Handbook 2026/27 (adapted)",
              text: "Applications for the part-time [[undergraduate course|grado / primer ciclo]] must be submitted by 17:00 on Friday 15 May. Late applications will not be considered. It should be noted that the deadline is a deadline: it is not a target and it is not a rumour. An application is understood to be complete only when the form, a [[personal statement|carta de motivación]] of 400 to 600 words, and evidence of a prior [[qualification|título o certificado]] have been received. Incomplete files are returned. They are not held.<br/><br/>[[Enrolment|la matrícula]] is not the same as application. An offer, if one is made, is sent in writing. The place is not confirmed until [[tuition fees|las tasas académicas]] for the first term have been paid, or a student-finance letter has been seen. It is widely believed, among applicants who have never been through the process, that an email of interest counts as a place. It does not. Word is not a source. A paid invoice is.<br/><br/>The course is taught over two evenings a week and one Saturday a month. One hundred and twenty [[credits|créditos]] must be completed for the award. Each twenty-credit module is assessed by [[coursework|trabajo de curso]], not by a single unseen exam. Work must be submitted by 23:59 on the published date. Late work is capped at the pass mark if it arrives within five working days; after that it is given a mark of zero. An [[extension|prórroga]] may be granted once, and only if a request is received at least three working days before the deadline, with evidence. «I was busy» is not evidence. A medical note is.<br/><br/>It is expected that students will attend seminars. Attendance is not a rumour on a group chat. It is recorded. A student who misses four seminars in a term is invited to a meeting. The meeting is not a punishment. It is a chance to say whether the course is still possible. Places on this programme are limited. A place that is not being used is a place that could have been offered to someone on the waiting list.<br/><br/>Recognition of prior learning is possible. Credits earned at another UK institution may be transferred if a transcript and a module description are provided. Credits from outside the UK are considered, not guaranteed. A [[degree|título universitario]] from another country is not automatically a degree here. The claim that «any university counts» has been denied in writing, more than once, by the admissions office.<br/><br/>Ms Kehinde Adeyemi, who signs the offers, was asked last year why the handbook is written in the passive. «Because the rule has no owner in the room,» she said. «If I write I will not mark late work, it becomes my mood. If the handbook writes late work will not be marked, it is the course. Who says so? The regulation. That is the point.»<br/><br/>Valeria Soto read the handbook on a Thursday, at the kitchen table, with a pencil. Amy, who trained as a nurse and who has sat through more handbooks than she cares to count, read the extension paragraph twice. «That's the sentence that catches people,» she said. «They think the deadline is a conversation. It is not. It is a date.» Nora, who was making tea, asked whether the college was the one by the library. It is. Applications are still being taken. The shop of rumours on Palmer Road has already decided that the course is full. That claim has not been independently verified. Until it is, the handbook is the source, and the handbook still says 15 May.",
